e-threat-modeling-concepts walkthrough log — 2026-10-10
Worked-case lesson: no command line by design (brief §6).
The "run" is the methodology-selection record authored and self-reviewed
in-session against four fetched methodology sources.

Sources fetched 2026-10-10 (all verified before writing):
  - Microsoft Threat Modeling docs / STRIDE (learn.microsoft.com, 50,208
    bytes): six categories confirmed (spoofing/tampering/repudiation text
    read; I/D/E per the standard mnemonic), per-component questioning method.
  - PASTA (versprite.com, 112,520 bytes): seven stages; Stage 1 Define
    Objectives (business-impact framing first); stages 4-6 Threat Analysis /
    Vulnerability & Weakness Analysis / Attack Modeling (threat-intel
    correlation + attack-path simulation); Stage 7.
  - OCTAVE: SEI library page 404'd (logged as unverified lead, not cited);
    recovered via SEI "Introduction to the OCTAVE Approach" (Aug 2003,
    sei.cmu.edu PDF, 448,179 bytes): three phases — asset-based threat
    profiles; infrastructure vulnerabilities; security strategy and plans.
  - Attack trees: Schneier "Attack Trees" (Dec 1999, schneier.com, 64,074
    bytes): goal-oriented AND/OR decomposition.
  - OWASP Threat Modeling Cheat Sheet (88,587 bytes): PASTA + OCTAVE
    expansions confirmed ("Process for Attack Simulation and Threat
    Analysis"; "Operationally Critical Threat, Asset, and Vulnerability
    Evaluation").

Scope: fictional Harbor Clinic; four situations -> STRIDE (portal in dev),
OCTAVE (standing up the practice), attack trees (EHR crown jewel), PASTA risk
framing (prioritizing combined output). Each row: rationale, staffing,
consumer, cadence. Differentiated from e-sdlc (hands-on STRIDE) and
ai-threat-modeling (AI variant) in §1/§2/§10.

